<section class="block-section story">
  <span class="section-tag">Story</span>
  <h2>What today is about</h2>
  <p class="sub">The move that makes this class different from most.</p>
  <p><strong>We open with what happened on your screens.</strong> Last night you logged into the class AI platform for the first time and ran the OPVL tool on <a href="/units/unit-0/block-2/">Lovelock (1965)</a>. Bring out your output, what was helpful, and where it was difficult. If you could not get in at all, that is the finding we want first, not something to be embarrassed about.</p>
  <p>Then we go further. AI outputs are sources, and they get the same OPVL framework and the same critical eye. By the end of today you will be holding <strong>four</strong> responses to one paper you have actually read: yours from last night, two that Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt ran on different models, and one more you run yourself. Same content, different source types, and they will not say the same things. The question you leave with is not which one is nicest to read. It is <strong>which one said anything specific enough that you could catch it being wrong</strong>. Then we introduce the habit you'll carry for the rest of the year: <strong>every time you use AI in your work, you fill out the AI Documentation Template</strong>.</p>
  <p>The documentation isn't there to police you. It's there to make you notice what the AI did versus what you did. Students who can articulate that distinction learn more from AI than students who can't. Honest disclosure = no penalty. Hidden AI use = academic integrity violation. That's the whole protocol. Want to see the protocol working on a real document? The <a href="/units/unit-0/exemplar-source-dossier/#ai-doc">exemplar dossier's own AI Documentation page</a> is a live example: the exemplar was built with AI and documents itself the same way you will.</p>
  <p>One more thing, said plainly: on our class AI platform, Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt can see student conversations. That's not surveillance of your thinking; it's the same principle as the Documentation Template. In this class, AI use is visible by design. Work as if your process will be read, because it will be.</p>
</section>

