<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work</h2>
  <p class="sub">One task today. Everyone runs the same comparison, because the whole point is that we can put our answers side by side.</p>
  <div class="choice-intro">
    <strong>Learning Intention:</strong> {{ learningIntention | safe }}
  </div>
  <div class="callout task">
    <div class="callout-title">Everyone does this</div>
    <p>Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt has already uploaded <strong>Lovelock (1965)</strong>, the paper you OPVL'd last class, and run the same prompt through two models: <strong>ChatGPT 4.1</strong> and <strong>Claude 4.8</strong>. You will add a third, and you already have a fourth from last night. Then they get checked against the paper. This is this morning's bell ringer again, except this time you are holding the primary source.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Get set up properly.</strong> See the box below for your section's platform. If you got in last night, you are done with this step in thirty seconds. If you did not, this is the fifteen minutes to fix it, and Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt will come to you.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the two you were given.</strong> The ChatGPT 4.1 and Claude 4.8 responses are on the <a href="/units/unit-0/readings/model-comparison/">Two models, one paper</a> page, in full. Do not skim them. You are looking for where they differ, and the page is searchable for a reason.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a third.</strong> Download the Lovelock PDF from Google Classroom, upload it to a new conversation, and pick a <strong>model nobody demonstrated</strong>. Run this prompt, word for word, so your output is comparable to the other two:<br>
        <em>"Summarise this paper. Then write an OPVL analysis of it: Origin, Purpose, Value, Limitation."</em></li>
      <li><strong>Compare at the claim level.</strong> Pull three specific claims from each response, including the one you ran at home last night. The same claim in different words is not a disagreement. Mark only where they genuinely differ, and mark what one says that the others leave out entirely.</li>
      <li><strong>Find something checkable and check it.</strong> Pick a claim specific enough to be verified: a date, a journal, a name, a chemical, a quoted phrase. <strong>Check it in the PDF</strong>, not the <a href="/units/unit-0/readings/lovelock-1965/">interactive reader</a>. The reader is shortened for reading support, so something missing from it may still be in the paper. Does the paper actually say that?</li>
      <li><strong>Document it.</strong> Full OPVL on your third response, the Limitation line on the others, and one <a href="/student-hub/ai-documentation-template/">AI Documentation Template</a> covering all of it.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Steps 1 and 3, on your section's platform</div>
    <p>Check Google Classroom for which platform your section uses. Sign in with your school account. This is the tool you will use for the rest of the year, so it is worth getting properly set up today rather than half-working.</p>
    <p><strong>On BoodleBox:</strong> open a new chat and upload the PDF there. For step 3, choose any model other than ChatGPT 4.1 or Claude 4.8 and run the prompt on that same uploaded paper. You are changing the tool and holding everything else constant.</p>
    <p><strong>On Flint:</strong> you cannot pick the model, so for step 3 run the prompt in a <strong>genuinely new conversation</strong> and upload the paper again. Do not type "do it again" in the same thread, because it will read its own first answer and agree with itself. You are changing nothing at all and seeing what happens anyway. If your run comes back nearly identical to itself, that is your result, and step 5 still applies.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">The thing to notice in step 4</div>
    <p>These responses do not contradict each other the way the two Cauvery answers did. Nothing here says "2007" against "2018". They differ in <strong>how specific they are willing to be</strong>. One names the journal, the volume, the grant number, and the exact chemicals. One says "the 1960s" and "several experimental approaches."</p>
    <p>So the question is not just "which one disagrees." It is: <strong>which one said enough to be caught being wrong?</strong> A response too vague to be checked is not a safer response. It is an unfalsifiable one, and that belongs in your Limitation line.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <div class="callout-title">What if all three basically agree?</div>
    <p>That is a real result, not a failed task. Write it down, then do step 5 anyway, because three responses agreeing tells you nothing about whether any of them is right. If they agree and they are all wrong about the same thing, you have found the most important idea in this block: <strong>consistency is not accuracy.</strong></p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout extend">
    <div class="callout-title">If you finish early</div>
    <p>Put today's OPVL beside your own OPVL of the paper from <a href="/units/unit-0/block-2/">Block 2</a>. What specifically is different in the Limitation line? Reading a paper and reading a summary of a paper are different acts, and the Limitation line is where that difference shows up.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">If you are stuck</div>
    <p>Work with a partner and open the sample AI Documentation entry from Google Classroom. It has some fields done well and some done badly. Use it as the model for what "specific" looks like, then come back to your own.</p>
  </div>
  {% include "partials/notebook-cue.njk" %}
